.TH "DRIFTSTAMP" "1" "" "Driftstamp 0.1.2" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
driftstamp \- rediscover machine customizations and prepare reviewed migrations
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.B "driftstamp [GLOBAL OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGUMENTS]"
.br
.B "driftstamp \-\-help"
.br
.B "driftstamp \-\-version"
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Discover forgotten customizations and prepare a reviewed migration bundle.
.PP
Driftstamp reads supported configuration files and literal references, saves immutable snapshots, and records your migration decisions separately. The initial discovery modules cover Linux i3, Bash/Zsh startup files, local text scripts, systemd units and drop\-ins, and cron. Built\-in destination adapters propose Ubuntu or Arch Linux setup steps.
.PP
Discovery does not execute scripts, source shell configuration, query active services, or change inspected source files. State and export commands write only their requested local outputs. A migration plan is a review document; it never installs packages, activates services, or applies destination changes.
.PP
Without an installation baseline, candidates and references cannot establish who authored a file or reconstruct deleted changes. Use saved snapshots to compare later observations.
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.PP
\-\-state\-dir, \-\-format, \-\-no\-color, and \-\-allow\-plugins are accepted before or after a subcommand. If repeated, the later value wins. \-\-version is accepted at the top level. \-\-help describes the parser where it appears.
.TP
.B "\-h, \-\-help"
show this help message and exit.
.TP
.B "\-\-state\-dir STATE_DIR"
directory for snapshots and review decisions.
.TP
.B "\-\-format {text,json}"
output format (default: text). Choices: text, json.
.TP
.B "\-\-no\-color"
disable color (output is currently always plain text).
.TP
.B "\-\-allow\-plugins"
load installed module and target plugins; executes trusted Python code.
.TP
.B "\-\-version"
show the program version and exit.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.PP
Each command accepts the common global options above. Saved\-scan readers use the latest completed snapshot by default and never silently rescan.
.SS "scan"
.B "driftstamp scan [GLOBAL OPTIONS] [\-\-root ROOT] [\-\-home HOME] [\-\-platform {linux,windows}] [\-\-distro DISTRO] [\-\-scope {user,system,all}] [\-\-module ID] [\-\-include PATH] [\-\-exclude GLOB] [\-\-name NAME]"
.PP
Discover customizations and save a new immutable snapshot.
.PP
All applicable built\-in modules and both user and system locations are selected by default. Access failures and unresolved references remain visible in the coverage report. Snapshot names are immutable: use a new name for a later scan. An alternate filesystem root requires \-\-home within that root. User and all scopes require an existing home directory. Directory exclusions also block referenced children and resolved symlink targets. Explicit includes add script locations and do not select files for migration. Quote exclusion globs to prevent expansion by your shell.
.TP
.B "\-\-root ROOT"
filesystem root; a fixture root requires \-\-home.
.TP
.B "\-\-home HOME"
physical home directory, located within \-\-root.
.TP
.B "\-\-platform {linux,windows}"
platform to inspect (default: current platform). Choices: linux, windows.
.TP
.B "\-\-distro DISTRO"
distribution identifier (default: detect from root).
.TP
.B "\-\-scope {user,system,all}"
locations to inspect (default: all); omitted areas remain coverage gaps. Choices: user, system, all.
.TP
.B "\-\-module ID"
run this module; repeat to select more.
.TP
.B "\-\-include PATH"
additional script discovery location; repeatable.
.TP
.B "\-\-exclude GLOB"
exclude matching paths; quote shell globs; repeatable.
.TP
.B "\-\-name NAME"
name for the saved snapshot.
.SS "list"
.B "driftstamp list [GLOBAL OPTIONS] [\-\-scan NAME] [\-\-module ID] [\-\-decision {undecided,keep,skip,investigate}] [\-\-min\-confidence 0..100] [\-\-needs\-review]"
.PP
List findings from a saved scan without rescanning.
.PP
Use the finding IDs printed by this command with show and review. Confidence filters select by evidence strength, not probability. \-\-needs\-review includes undecided and investigate findings; other filters are combined with it.
.TP
.B "\-\-scan NAME"
saved snapshot to read (default: latest).
.TP
.B "\-\-module ID"
show findings from this module; repeatable.
.TP
.B "\-\-decision {undecided,keep,skip,investigate}"
show findings with this migration decision. Choices: undecided, keep, skip, investigate.
.TP
.B "\-\-min\-confidence 0..100"
minimum uncalibrated evidence score (default: 0).
.TP
.B "\-\-needs\-review"
show undecided or investigate findings.
.SS "show"
.B "driftstamp show [GLOBAL OPTIONS] ID [\-\-scan NAME]"
.PP
Explain one finding, with its files, evidence, dependencies, and warnings.
.PP
Displays the finding's resources, fingerprints, evidence, dependencies, warnings, migration hint, decision and note. This command does not print raw file contents or a vendor\-baseline diff.
.TP
.B "ID"
finding ID from list.
.TP
.B "\-\-scan NAME"
saved snapshot to read (default: latest).
.SS "review"
.B "driftstamp review [GLOBAL OPTIONS] ID \-\-decision {undecided,keep,skip,investigate} [\-\-note NOTE]"
.PP
Record a migration decision and optional note for one finding.
.PP
The finding must exist in the latest snapshot. Decisions and notes persist across scans for the same stable finding ID. Omitting \-\-note preserves an existing note; an empty string clears it. keep selects a finding for export; skip leaves it in the inventory without selecting it for export. undecided and investigate remain reviewable.
.TP
.B "ID"
finding ID in the latest snapshot.
.TP
.B "\-\-decision {undecided,keep,skip,investigate}"
migration decision to save; skip does not hide a discovery finding. Required. Choices: undecided, keep, skip, investigate.
.TP
.B "\-\-note NOTE"
why this customization exists or should be kept.
.SS "diff"
.B "driftstamp diff [GLOBAL OPTIONS] OLD [NEW]"
.PP
Compare saved snapshots, accounting for unscanned areas.
.PP
Compares saved fingerprints, file modes, links and discovery scope. Areas that were not inspected are reported as comparison gaps rather than assumed deletions.
.TP
.B "OLD"
earlier saved snapshot name.
.TP
.B "[NEW]"
later snapshot name (default: latest).
.SS "coverage"
.B "driftstamp coverage [GLOBAL OPTIONS] [\-\-scan NAME]"
.PP
Show declared\-check coverage and every coverage gap.
.PP
Lists every declared check and its status, including unsupported, excluded, blocked and partial areas. See CONFIDENCE for the scoring formula.
.TP
.B "\-\-scan NAME"
saved snapshot to read (default: latest).
.SS "modules"
.B "driftstamp modules [GLOBAL OPTIONS]"
.PP
List available discovery modules and platform support.
.PP
Lists available discovery module IDs, their platform support and declared checks. Installed external modules are loaded only with \-\-allow\-plugins.
.SS "export"
.B "driftstamp export [GLOBAL OPTIONS] [\-\-scan NAME] \-\-output PATH"
.PP
Export findings marked keep; verify their files have not changed.
.PP
Exports only findings currently marked keep and verifies each source's content or link target and mode against the selected snapshot. Missing or changed sources fail visibly; rescan and review before trying again. The output directory must not already exist. An empty selection is an error. The bundle includes selected content, hashes, mode and link metadata, evidence and notes. Links are recorded, not created. Contents are not redacted and may contain credentials.
.TP
.B "\-\-scan NAME"
saved snapshot to export (default: latest).
.TP
.B "\-\-output PATH"
new bundle directory; must not already exist. Required.
.SS "plan"
.B "driftstamp plan [GLOBAL OPTIONS] BUNDLE \-\-target TARGET [\-\-release RELEASE] [\-\-check\-host]"
.PP
Inspect a migration bundle and print setup instructions; never apply them.
.PP
Validates the bundle and proposes package arguments and copy, adapt, merge or review or omit actions. Unknown dependencies remain unresolved. \-\-release records the requested release; built\-in package mappings are not release\-specific and package repository availability is not verified. \-\-check\-host also inspects the current destination for platform compatibility and file conflicts. No plan is applied.
.TP
.B "BUNDLE"
exported bundle directory containing manifest.json.
.TP
.B "\-\-target TARGET"
destination adapter (built in: ubuntu, arch; plugins may add more). Required.
.TP
.B "\-\-release RELEASE"
destination release identifier.
.TP
.B "\-\-check\-host"
also inspect the current destination host for conflicts.
.SS "snapshots"
.B "driftstamp snapshots [GLOBAL OPTIONS]"
.PP
List saved snapshot names.
.PP
Prints saved snapshot names in sorted order; this does not inspect source files.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
Discover, inspect, and select a finding. Replace FINDING_ID with an actual ID from list.
.nf
driftstamp scan \-\-name before\-cleanup
driftstamp list \-\-needs\-review
driftstamp show FINDING_ID
driftstamp review FINDING_ID \-\-decision keep \-\-note \(dqNeeded for my desk\(dq
driftstamp export \-\-output ./my\-setup
driftstamp plan ./my\-setup \-\-target arch
.fi
.PP
Limit discovery and produce structured output:
.nf
driftstamp scan \-\-scope user \-\-module i3 \-\-module systemd
driftstamp scan \-\-include ~/scripts \-\-exclude '~/scripts/old/**'
driftstamp list \-\-format json
driftstamp coverage \-\-scan before\-cleanup
driftstamp diff before\-cleanup latest
.fi
.PP
Inspect an isolated fixture without inspecting your real home:
.nf
driftstamp \-\-state\-dir ./fixture\-state scan \-\-root ./fixture/root \-\-home ./fixture/root/home/alice \-\-distro ubuntu
.fi
.SH "INSTALLATION"
.PP
The source distribution includes tools/install_user.py. Pass a trusted built Driftstamp wheel to install into a private POSIX environment under ~/.local/share/driftstamp, expose ~/.local/bin/driftstamp, and install this manual under ~/.local/share/man/man1. \-\-prefix selects another user prefix. The command directory must be on PATH. The installer verifies a new environment before switching the command link and retains earlier environments. See README.md for offline build prerequisites and installation commands.
.SH "FILES"
.PP
\-\-state\-dir overrides the state directory. Paths below are symbolic, not values obtained from the build machine.
.TP
.B "$XDG_STATE_HOME/driftstamp/"
Default Linux state directory when XDG_STATE_HOME is set. Otherwise use ~/.local/state/driftstamp/. Under sudo, use the original caller's ~/.local/state/driftstamp/ and ignore an inherited XDG_STATE_HOME.
.TP
.B "%LOCALAPPDATA%/driftstamp/"
Default Windows state directory; falls back to ~/AppData/Local/driftstamp/ when LOCALAPPDATA is unset. Native Windows discovery is not implemented by the built\-in modules.
.TP
.B "STATE_DIR/snapshots/NAME.json"
Versioned immutable snapshot records.
.TP
.B "STATE_DIR/latest.json"
Reference to the latest saved snapshot.
.TP
.B "STATE_DIR/reviews.json"
Persistent decisions and notes keyed by finding ID.
.TP
.B "BUNDLE/manifest.json"
Versioned export manifest, selected findings and resource metadata.
.TP
.B "BUNDLE/blobs/SHA256"
Bundled regular\-file contents addressed by their SHA\-256 digest.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
.B "XDG_STATE_HOME"
Linux state base; ignored for a sudo caller unless \-\-state\-dir is explicit.
.TP
.B "LOCALAPPDATA"
Windows state base.
.TP
.B "HOME, USERPROFILE"
Used by Python's platform\-specific home\-directory lookup when \-\-home is omitted. A sudo caller is resolved through the account database instead.
.TP
.B "SUDO_UID"
On a privileged POSIX invocation, identifies the original user for home and default\-state selection. If the account cannot be resolved, pass \-\-home and \-\-state\-dir explicitly.
.PP
The built\-in collectors use conventional configuration locations. Custom XDG_CONFIG_HOME and ZDOTDIR layouts are not automatically discovered.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B "0"
Operation completed, including \-\-help and \-\-version. Unsupported or explicitly excluded checks alone do not change this status.
.TP
.B "1"
Runtime, filesystem, snapshot or bundle\-data failure.
.TP
.B "2"
Invalid command\-line arguments or argument combinations.
.TP
.B "3"
A scan was saved, but at least one declared check is blocked or partial.
.PP
Reports go to standard output; diagnostics go to standard error. \-\-format json keeps successful report output machine\-readable. A successful exit code is not a claim that every customization was found.
.SH "CONFIDENCE"
.PP
Finding confidence is an uncalibrated evidence\-strength score from 0 to 100. Inspect the associated evidence and warnings. It is not a probability of human authorship, completeness or safe migration.
.PP
Declared\-check coverage is 100 times the sum of completed check weights divided by the sum of all applicable check weights. not_applicable checks are omitted from the denominator. Partial, blocked, unsupported and excluded checks remain in the denominator and receive no completion credit. An empty denominator is reported as unavailable, not 100 percent.
.PP
Software test results are a separate measurement. Passing reproducible tests does not measure recall on real machines or prove a migration will succeed.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
.PP
Current discovery follows supported literal references without evaluating shell expressions, i3 variables, arbitrary script bodies or active service state. Some dependencies remain unresolved. Regular\-file reads are limited to 1 MiB and directory traversal to 2,000 entries per discovery root. Directory symlinks are not traversed. Failures and scope gaps remain visible.
.PP
Package configuration baselines, arbitrary application preferences, ACLs, capabilities and ownership comparison remain unsupported. A configuration candidate does not prove a modification from its packaged default. Native PowerShell, CMD, Windows services/tasks and dedicated RPM/Red Hat collectors require future modules.
.PP
Migration is deliberately reviewed. Hardware identifiers, usernames, paths, credentials and distribution defaults can differ on the destination. Exported content is local and unredacted; review it before sharing. This release does not implement unattended restoration or continuous background monitoring.
.SH "PLUGINS"
.PP
Discovery modules use the driftstamp.modules Python entry\-point group; destination adapters use driftstamp.targets. External plugins are disabled unless \-\-allow\-plugins is supplied. Enabling them executes their Python code with the same privileges as Driftstamp; load only trusted packages.
.PP
Plugins declare an API version and capabilities. Discovery module failures and unsupported areas must remain visible rather than implying successful inspection. A new discovery resource kind also needs explicit export and planning support before it can be migrated. See docs/PLUGINS.md in the source distribution for the contract and the Red Hat extension example.
